# Bootstrap from zero

Bring up the full stack on a fresh server, top to bottom. Every step has a
verify command — do not continue past a failing verify. This runbook covers
exactly the residue CI cannot test: the k3s install, systemd, hostPort on a
real NIC, real DNS and Let's Encrypt.

Total time: ~30–45 minutes, most of it waiting for ZITADEL's first start.

## 0. Prerequisites

- A server (Ubuntu or Debian, {{ target_arch }}) reachable at `{{ server_ipv4 }}`,
  root access, ports 80/443 free (no other web server running).
- DNS: `*.{{ domain }}` (wildcard A record) → `{{ server_ipv4 }}`.
- This repo pushed to `https://github.com/{{ github_owner }}/{{ project_name }}`.
- **On the server: `git`, `curl`, `unzip`, `gnupg`, `jq`.** A minimal
  Debian/Ubuntu image ships none of them (verified on Debian 13 trixie), and
  each bites in a different step: §1 pipes `get.k3s.io` through `curl`, §3–§4
  clone this repo and push the sealed secrets back from the server, §5 installs
  OpenTofu (`unzip` + `gnupg`), and the verify commands in §2/§5 parse JSON with
  `jq`. Install them all up front, in one go:

  ```bash
  sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get install -y git curl unzip gnupg jq
  ```
- Locally (or on the agent's machine): `kubectl`, `kubeseal`, `tofu`, `git`
  (the `argocd` CLI is handy but optional — everything works via `kubectl`).

**Verify DNS before anything else:**

```bash
dig +short id.{{ domain }} anything.{{ domain }}
# both → {{ server_ipv4 }}
```

**Verify the server's tooling:**

```bash
for t in git curl unzip gpg jq; do
  command -v "$t" >/dev/null || echo "MISSING: $t"
done   # prints nothing when all of them are present
```

## 1. Install k3s and close the host

### 1.1 Install k3s

On the server, as root:

```bash
curl -sfL https://get.k3s.io | INSTALL_K3S_EXEC="--disable=traefik --disable=servicelb" sh -
```

The bundled Traefik and servicelb MUST stay disabled — `cluster/traefik/`
deploys our own Traefik with hostPort 80/443.

Kubeconfig for your user:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/.kube
sudo cp /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml ~/.kube/config
sudo chown "$USER:$USER" ~/.kube/config && chmod 600 ~/.kube/config
export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/config
```

**Verify:**

```bash
kubectl get node   # one node, STATUS Ready
```

To operate remotely, add a `tls-san` for your API domain in
`/etc/rancher/k3s/config.yaml` and open 6443 in the §1.2 guard (see §7); for a
first bootstrap, running directly on the server is fine.

### 1.2 Host firewall

Do this **now**, not later: between the k3s install and this step, the cluster
API is on the public internet.

k3s opens these on all interfaces, without asking:

| Port | Service |
|---|---|
| 6443/tcp | kube-apiserver |
| 10250/tcp | kubelet API |
| 10248, 10249, 10256–10259/tcp | component ports (bound to 127.0.0.1, listed for completeness) |
| 8472/udp | flannel VXLAN — **unauthenticated** |

VXLAN is the serious one: whoever can send UDP 8472 to the node injects packets
straight into the pod network, bypassing Traefik, ForwardAuth and ZITADEL
entirely.

**`ufw` does not close these — and it looks like it does, which is worse.** k3s
inserts its chains ahead of ufw's and accepts marked traffic before ufw is ever
reached. The INPUT chain after `ufw enable`:

```
-P INPUT DROP
-A INPUT ... -j KUBE-ROUTER-INPUT
-A INPUT ... -j KUBE-PROXY-FIREWALL
-A INPUT ... -j KUBE-NODEPORTS
-A INPUT ... -j KUBE-EXTERNAL-SERVICES
-A INPUT -j KUBE-FIREWALL
-A INPUT ... -m mark --mark 0x20000/0x20000 -j ACCEPT   <-- accepted here already
-A INPUT -j ufw-before-input                            <-- ufw only runs here
```

With `ufw default deny incoming` set and `ufw status` looking correct, 6443 and
10250 stay reachable from the internet — measured from an external host, not
read off the ruleset. Inserting plain iptables rules is no answer either: k3s
rewrites its chains regularly and they disappear.

**What works** is an own nftables table hooked *before* the filter table. It
runs ahead of the k3s chains and survives k3s rewriting its own rules:

```bash
sudo apt-get install -y nftables
sudo mkdir -p /etc/nftables.d
sudo tee /etc/nftables.d/k3sguard.nft >/dev/null <<'EOF'
#!/usr/sbin/nft -f
# Deliberately NO `flush ruleset` here — that would also wipe k3s's own
# iptables-nft chains until it resyncs. Recreate only our own table.
table inet k3sguard
delete table inet k3sguard

table inet k3sguard {
    chain input {
        type filter hook input priority -150; policy accept;
        iif lo accept
        ct state established,related accept
        ip saddr { 127.0.0.0/8, 10.42.0.0/16, 10.43.0.0/16, {{ server_ipv4 }} } accept
        iifname { "cni0", "flannel.1" } accept
        # Operating kubectl from your laptop instead of the server? Add an
        # accept for your admin IP ABOVE the drop, e.g.
        #   ip saddr <your-admin-ip> tcp dport 6443 accept
        # For the optional CI-OIDC case, open 6443 as described in §7.
        tcp dport { 6443, 10248, 10249, 10250, 10256, 10257, 10258, 10259 } drop
        udp dport 8472 drop
    }
}
EOF
grep -q 'nftables.d' /etc/nftables.conf \
  || echo 'include "/etc/nftables.d/*.nft"' | sudo tee -a /etc/nftables.conf
sudo systemctl enable --now nftables
sudo nft -f /etc/nftables.conf
```

Re-running the file is safe: the `table` / `delete table` / `table { … }` idiom
recreates only our own table, so rules never accumulate. `nft list ruleset`
prints the priority as `mangle` (= -150) and may collapse the two CIDRs into
`10.42.0.0/15` — both are display forms of what you wrote, not a modification.

`policy accept` plus an explicit drop list is deliberate: this table closes the
k3s ports and nothing else, so 22/80/443 stay open and it cannot lock you out
of SSH. It is a guard, not a general firewall. `10.42.0.0/16` and
`10.43.0.0/16` are k3s's default pod and service CIDRs; the node's own address
must stay in the accept set, or the apiserver can no longer reach the kubelet
and `kubectl logs`/`exec` break.

If you want ufw on top (cosmetic here — the nft table does the work), set
`DEFAULT_FORWARD_POLICY="ACCEPT"` in `/etc/default/ufw` first, otherwise the
pod network breaks.

**Verify — from an external host. Testing from the server proves nothing:**

```bash
# On a DIFFERENT machine, out on the public internet:
nc -vz -w3 {{ server_ipv4 }} 443     # succeeds
nc -vz -w3 {{ server_ipv4 }} 6443    # must time out
nc -vz -w3 {{ server_ipv4 }} 10250   # must time out
sudo nmap -Pn -sU -p 8472 {{ server_ipv4 }}   # must not be open
```

Two traps here look like results and are not:

- **Behind an egress proxy, every port reports open.** If the machine you scan
  from routes through a proxy (corporate network, CI runner, sandboxed
  container with `HTTP(S)_PROXY` set), the proxy accepts the TCP connection
  locally and every port comes back open. Self-check first:
  `nc -vz -w3 {{ server_ipv4 }} 6444` — nothing listens there, so if *that*
  reads open you are measuring the proxy, not the server. Use a real external
  host.
- **A failing `kubectl exec` is not automatically a firewall problem.** Later
  in the bootstrap, `kubectl exec` into ZITADEL fails because its distroless
  image has no `sh`/`echo` — the error reads like a broken apiserver→kubelet
  path but is only the image. Test that path with busybox instead.

**Verify the cluster still works from the server:**

```bash
kubectl get node        # still Ready
kubectl run fwcheck --rm -it --restart=Never --image=busybox:1.36 -- echo ok
# ^ exercises the apiserver → kubelet path the node-IP accept rule keeps open
```

## 2. Install Argo CD

Argo CD is the one component installed imperatively — everything after it comes
from git. **Do not pin an old release.** An Argo CD older than the cluster's
Kubernetes version cannot build a typed diff against newer built-in schemas and
stops reconciling *while still reporting Healthy*
(`runbooks/incidents/argocd-version-skew.md`). `stable` is the safe default; a
pinned version is a value you must re-check against the k3s version from §1 on
every rebuild.

```bash
# REVIEWED VALUE — check on every rebuild. `stable` tracks the current release.
# If you pin a tag (e.g. v3.4.6) instead, it MUST be new enough for the
# Kubernetes version installed in §1 — newer is fine, older is not.
ARGOCD_VERSION=stable

kubectl create namespace argocd
kubectl apply -n argocd \
  -f "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-cd/$ARGOCD_VERSION/manifests/install.yaml"

# Two argocd-cm tweaks this single-node, no-LoadBalancer stack needs (see
# runbooks/incidents/argocd-resource-tracking.md):
#  1. annotation-based resource tracking — the ZITADEL chart provisions runtime
#     secrets labelled app.kubernetes.io/instance=zitadel; Argo's DEFAULT label
#     tracking mistakes them for orphans and prunes them (login-client →
#     zitadel-login never starts).
#  2. custom Ingress health — Traefik runs ClusterIP+hostPort with no
#     LoadBalancer, so Ingresses never get .status.loadBalancer; Argo's default
#     health check would otherwise hang every Ingress at Progressing forever.
kubectl -n argocd patch configmap argocd-cm --type merge -p '{"data":{
  "application.resourceTrackingMethod":"annotation",
  "resource.customizations.health.networking.k8s.io_Ingress":"hs = {} hs.status = \"Healthy\" hs.message = \"single-node hostPort Traefik publishes no load-balancer status\" return hs"
}}'
kubectl -n argocd rollout restart statefulset/argocd-application-controller
```

`install.yaml` **contains `argocd-cm`**: whenever you (re-)apply it — first
install, and every later upgrade — the two patches above are overwritten and
must be applied again. Upgrading and forgetting them silently re-introduces
both traps from `runbooks/incidents/argocd-resource-tracking.md`.

**Verify:**

```bash
kubectl -n argocd rollout status deploy/argocd-repo-server --timeout=300s
kubectl -n argocd get pods   # server, repo-server, application/applicationset controllers Running
# Argo CD version vs. cluster version — Argo must not be the older of the two.
kubectl -n argocd get deploy argocd-server \
  -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}{"\n"}'
kubectl version -o json | jq -r .serverVersion.gitVersion
# The patches survived the apply (both keys present):
kubectl -n argocd get cm argocd-cm -o jsonpath='{.data}' | tr ',' '\n' | grep -E 'resourceTrackingMethod|Ingress'
```

## 3. Bootstrap Argo CD from this repo

If the repo is private, first register the **GitHub App** repo credential for
the org that owns it — NEVER a PAT (invariant 4), created directly in the
cluster, never committed (invariant 3). Full recipe in
`runbooks/argocd-github-app.md`; in short, for `{{ github_owner }}`:

```bash
INST=$(gh api /orgs/{{ github_owner }}/installations \
  --jq ".installations[]|select(.app_id==<app-id>).id")
kubectl create secret generic {{ github_owner }}-argocd-secret -n argocd \
  --from-literal=type=git \
  --from-literal=url=https://github.com/{{ github_owner }} \
  --from-literal=githubAppID=<app-id> \
  --from-literal=githubAppInstallationID="$INST" \
  --from-file=githubAppPrivateKey=<app-key.pem>
kubectl label secret {{ github_owner }}-argocd-secret -n argocd \
  argocd.argoproj.io/secret-type=repo-creds
```

(A PUBLIC repo needs none of this.) Then — the only manual `kubectl apply` in
the lifetime of this cluster — apply the root "app of apps":

```bash
kubectl apply -k argocd/
```

Argo CD now reconciles the whole substrate from git (Traefik, cert-manager,
sealed-secrets, rbac, policy, then ZITADEL and apps), ordered by sync waves.

**Verify (cert-manager CRDs take a minute):**

```bash
kubectl -n argocd get applications
# root:               Synced/Healthy
# traefik:            Synced/Healthy
# cluster:            Synced/Healthy   (cert-manager, sealed-secrets, rbac, policy)
# zitadel-base:       OutOfSync        ← expected! sealed secrets missing, next step
# zitadel-postgresql: Progressing → Healthy
# zitadel:            Progressing      ← waits on the sealed secrets too
# apps:               Synced/Healthy   (empty)
kubectl -n kube-system get ds traefik    # DESIRED 1, READY 1
curl -sI http://{{ domain }}             # answers (404 is fine — Traefik is up)
```

An Application showing `SYNC: Unknown` while `HEALTH: Healthy` is **not** fine —
that is the version-skew failure from §2. Read the reason, do not continue:

```bash
kubectl -n argocd get app <name> -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions}'
# "field not declared in schema" → Argo CD is too old for this cluster,
# see runbooks/incidents/argocd-version-skew.md
```

## 4. Seal the ZITADEL bootstrap secrets

```bash
./scripts/fetch-sealing-cert.sh
./scripts/seal-zitadel-secrets.sh
git add sealed-secrets-public-key.pem zitadel/sealed-zitadel-*.yaml
git commit -m "Seal ZITADEL bootstrap secrets" && git push
# Argo CD picks this up on its next poll (~3 min); to apply immediately:
kubectl -n argocd patch app zitadel-base --type merge \
  -p '{"operation":{"sync":{}}}'   # or: argocd app sync zitadel-base
```

**Verify (first start takes 5–15 min — init and setup jobs run first):**

```bash
kubectl -n argocd get app zitadel-base zitadel-postgresql zitadel  # → Synced/Healthy
kubectl -n zitadel get pods             # zitadel + zitadel-login Running
curl -fsS https://id.{{ domain }}/.well-known/openid-configuration | head -3
```

If the ZITADEL sync hangs in install >30s: pull the init pod logs IMMEDIATELY —
`runbooks/incidents/zitadel-bootstrap.md` (lesson learned section). Do not wait.

**Verify TLS is real:**

```bash
kubectl get certificates -A             # all Ready=True
curl -fsSI https://id.{{ domain }} > /dev/null && echo TLS OK
```

> **Do not pause here.** The moment this verify passes, ZITADEL is on the
> public internet **with an active IAM_OWNER account whose password is in the
> vendor's documentation** — you did not configure it, ZITADEL created it from
> its defaults. §5.1 + §5.2 close that window and are part of *this* sitting,
> not of "tomorrow's identity work"
> (`runbooks/incidents/zitadel-default-admin.md`, invariant 9).

## 5. ZITADEL bootstrap (instance/org only — NOT identity content)

This stands up the org and the first admin user. **Identity content** —
projects, roles, OIDC clients and above all who is in which role — is **not**
done here: it is API-managed at runtime (invariant 6,
`runbooks/zitadel-identity-via-api.md`). Do not commit memberships to tofu.

**No human console step is needed here.** ZITADEL's setup job has already
created the machine user `iam-admin` and stored its **JWT profile** as the k8s
secret `zitadel/iam-admin`, and the provider authenticates with
`jwt_profile_file` (`tofu/zitadel/main.tf`). Use that credential **once**, then
revoke it: §5.4 is a mandatory part of this step, not a cleanup footnote. The
invariant forbids a *standing* provider credential on disk or in the cluster —
using the chart's key for one apply and revoking it immediately satisfies it
more strictly than a hand-minted PAT that stays valid until someone remembers
it (invariants 3 + 6, `tofu/zitadel/README.md`).

**Order in this section is load-bearing.** §5.2 (inventory + remove the vendor
default admin) comes first because it is the only step with an exposure window,
and §5.4 (revoke) comes last because it removes the credential every earlier
step needs. Do not reorder them, and do not defer §5.2 "until the rest works" —
that is exactly how an IAM_OWNER with a published default password stayed live
on the internet for hours (`runbooks/incidents/zitadel-default-admin.md`).

### 5.1 Take the credential out for this session

```bash
kubectl create namespace terraform-state
cd tofu/zitadel
umask 077       # the credential must not be world-readable, not even briefly
kubectl -n zitadel get secret iam-admin \
  -o jsonpath='{.data.iam-admin\.json}' | base64 -d > service-user.json
chmod 600 service-user.json
```

`service-user.json` is the default of `var.jwt_profile_file` and is gitignored
(`tofu/zitadel/.gitignore`) — never commit it, never copy it elsewhere.

**Verify:**

```bash
jq -re '.userId, .keyId' service-user.json   # both non-empty
git check-ignore -v service-user.json        # must print a .gitignore rule
```

### 5.2 Instance administrators: list them, then kill the vendor default

**Do this before anything else touches the instance, and do not walk away
between §4 and the verify at the end of this step.** Until it passes,
`https://id.{{ domain }}` is a publicly reachable login form for an **IAM_OWNER
account whose password is printed in the vendor's documentation** — the whole
instance, not just the community org.

`argocd/applications/zitadel.yaml` deliberately configures **no**
`FirstInstance.Org.Human` — so that no plaintext password ever sits in this
repo. That intent is right, and the conclusion drawn from it is wrong:
**leaving the value out does not mean no human admin is created.** ZITADEL
falls back to its own published defaults (`cmd/setup/steps.yaml` upstream) and
creates one anyway:

| | |
|---|---|
| Login name | `zitadel-admin@zitadel.id.{{ domain }}` (username + default org `ZITADEL` + `ExternalDomain`) |
| Password | `Password1!` — the documented upstream default |
| Instance role | `IAM_OWNER` |
| State | active, email pre-verified |

`PasswordChangeRequired: true` is **not** a mitigation. It only forces *the
first person who logs in* to pick a new password — and nothing guarantees that
person is you. On the open internet it is a race, and the attacker who wins it
does not get a warning banner, they get the instance.

Helpers for this step and §5.4 (need `jq` and `openssl`; `$PROFILE` is the file
from §5.1) — define them once, in the shell you keep for the rest of §5:

```bash
ZID=https://id.{{ domain }}
PROFILE=./service-user.json
b64url() { openssl base64 -A | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '='; }
zitadel_jwt() {   # RS256, signed with the key from the JWT profile
  local now kid uid hdr claims si
  now=$(date +%s)
  kid=$(jq -r .keyId "$PROFILE"); uid=$(jq -r .userId "$PROFILE")
  (umask 077; jq -r .key "$PROFILE" > "$PROFILE.pem")
  hdr=$(printf '{"alg":"RS256","typ":"JWT","kid":"%s"}' "$kid" | b64url)
  claims=$(printf '{"iss":"%s","sub":"%s","aud":"%s","iat":%s,"exp":%s}' \
    "$uid" "$uid" "$ZID" "$now" "$((now + 300))" | b64url)
  si="$hdr.$claims"
  printf '%s.%s' "$si" \
    "$(printf '%s' "$si" | openssl dgst -sha256 -sign "$PROFILE.pem" | b64url)"
}
zitadel_token() {   # raw token response — JSON with access_token, or an error
  curl -sS -X POST "$ZID/oauth/v2/token" \
    -d grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:jwt-bearer \
    --data-urlencode "assertion=$(zitadel_jwt)" \
    --data-urlencode 'scope=openid urn:zitadel:iam:org:project:id:zitadel:aud'
}
TOKEN=$(zitadel_token | jq -r .access_token)
test -n "$TOKEN" && [ "$TOKEN" != null ]   # else: no instance credential, see the fallback below
```

**1. List who actually holds instance-level rights.** Do not skip this because
you "know" — the entire point of the incident is that the list contained an
account nobody had ever heard of:

```bash
curl -fsS -X POST "$ZID/admin/v1/members/_search" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"query":{"limit":100}}' \
  | jq -r '.result[] | [.userId, .preferredLoginName, (.roles|join(","))] | @tsv'
```

A fresh instance answers with exactly three rows: `iam-admin` (machine,
`IAM_OWNER`, the bootstrap credential from §5.1), `login-client` (machine,
`IAM_LOGIN_CLIENT`, used by the login UI pod) — and
`zitadel-admin@zitadel.id.{{ domain }}` (human, `IAM_OWNER`), which nothing in
this repo asked for. Anything beyond those three on a fresh instance is an
incident, not a curiosity.

**Fallback — inventory with no ZITADEL credential at all.** This is the version
that matters after §5.4, when `iam-admin` is gone: the projection table is
readable straight from Postgres, so you can always answer "who administers this
instance?" even when you cannot log in. (The ZITADEL pod is distroless and has
no shell; the Postgres pod does — `exec` into that one.)

```bash
PGPW=$(kubectl -n zitadel get secret zitadel-db-credentials \
  -o jsonpath='{.data.postgresPassword}' | base64 -d)
kubectl -n zitadel exec -i statefulset/zitadel-postgresql -- \
  env PGPASSWORD="$PGPW" psql -U postgres -d zitadel -At -F'|' -c "
    SELECT m.user_id,
           COALESCE(l.login_name, u.username),
           CASE u.type  WHEN 1 THEN 'human' WHEN 2 THEN 'machine' ELSE '?' END,
           CASE u.state WHEN 1 THEN 'active'  WHEN 2 THEN 'inactive'
                        WHEN 3 THEN 'deleted' WHEN 4 THEN 'locked'
                        WHEN 5 THEN 'suspend' WHEN 6 THEN 'initial' ELSE '?' END,
           array_to_string(m.roles, ',')
      FROM projections.instance_members4 m
      JOIN projections.users14 u
        ON u.id = m.user_id AND u.instance_id = m.instance_id
      LEFT JOIN projections.login_names3 l
        ON l.user_id = m.user_id AND l.instance_id = m.instance_id AND l.is_primary
     ORDER BY 3, 2;"
```

The numeric suffixes (`…members4`, `users14`, `login_names3`) are ZITADEL's
projection versions and change between major releases. If a relation is not
found, discover the current names first instead of guessing:

```sql
SELECT table_name FROM information_schema.tables
 WHERE table_schema = 'projections'
   AND (table_name LIKE 'instance_members%' OR table_name LIKE 'users%'
        OR table_name LIKE 'login_names%')
 ORDER BY table_name;
```

**2. Change that password now — same session, no "later".**

```bash
ADMIN_LOGIN="zitadel-admin@zitadel.id.{{ domain }}"
ADMIN_ID=$(curl -fsS -X POST "$ZID/admin/v1/members/_search" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"query":{"limit":100}}' \
  | jq -r --arg l "$ADMIN_LOGIN" '.result[]|select(.preferredLoginName==$l)|.userId')
test -n "$ADMIN_ID"

# Random, and satisfying ZITADEL's default complexity policy (upper/lower/
# digit/symbol) by construction.
NEW_PW="$(openssl rand -base64 24 | tr -d '/+=')Aa1!"

curl -fsS -X POST "$ZID/v2/users/$ADMIN_ID/password" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d "$(jq -n --arg p "$NEW_PW" '{newPassword:{password:$p,changeRequired:false}}')"

printf 'store this in the password manager NOW: %s\n' "$NEW_PW"
```

Put it in the password manager **before** you run the verify — it is not
recoverable, and this account is about to be the instance's only human
IAM_OWNER (§5.4). Record its login name next to it: the whole failure mode is
an admin account nobody can name.

**3. Verify — a FAILED login with the default value.** "I changed the password"
is not a verify. The only evidence that counts is ZITADEL refusing
`Password1!`. The session API performs a real password check, so it can be
scripted:

```bash
probe_password() {   # prints the HTTP status: 201 = this password works
  curl -s -o /tmp/pwprobe.json -w '%{http_code}' -X POST "$ZID/v2/sessions" \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
    -d "$(jq -n --arg u "$1" --arg p "$2" \
          '{checks:{user:{loginName:$u},password:{password:$p}},lifetime:"60s"}')"
}

# Positive control FIRST — otherwise a typo in the login name gives you a
# green negative test that proves nothing.
[ "$(probe_password "$ADMIN_LOGIN" "$NEW_PW")" = "201" ] \
  || { echo "control failed: the NEW password does not work either — the"; \
       echo "negative test below would be meaningless. Fix this first."; }

# The actual assertion.
CODE=$(probe_password "$ADMIN_LOGIN" 'Password1!')
cat /tmp/pwprobe.json; echo
[ "$CODE" = "201" ] \
  && echo "FAIL: the vendor default still authenticates — DO NOT CONTINUE" \
  || echo "verified: 'Password1!' is rejected (HTTP $CODE)"
```

A pass is ZITADEL answering with a password-check error (`COMMAND-3M0fs`), not
an empty body or a `404` — those mean the request never reached the check, and
the positive control above is what tells the two apart.

Sessions carry `lifetime: 60s` so the probes expire on their own. The default
lockout policy allows unlimited attempts, so two probes cannot lock the
account; if you have already tightened it, `_search` the lockout policy first.

**4. Optional but recommended:** once §5.4 has given *your own* account
IAM_OWNER, deactivate this one — an admin account with no owner is a liability
even with a good password:

```bash
curl -fsS -X POST "$ZID/v2/users/$ADMIN_ID/deactivate" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{}'
```

Do **not** do this before §5.4 succeeds: right now it is the only human
IAM_OWNER that exists.

### 5.3 Apply the bootstrap

If OpenTofu is not on the server yet:

```bash
curl -fsSL https://get.opentofu.org/install-opentofu.sh -o /tmp/install-opentofu.sh
chmod +x /tmp/install-opentofu.sh && /tmp/install-opentofu.sh --install-method standalone
```

> The installer needs `unzip` and `gnupg` (§0). Without them it exits 1 after
> printing a single line — `Unzip is missing, please install it to use the
> standalone installation method.` — and nothing else. Run from a background
> job or a script, all you see is "exit code 1"; do not go hunting elsewhere,
> just install the packages from §0.

```bash
tofu init && tofu apply    # provider reads ./service-user.json
```

**Verify:**

```bash
tofu version                               # installer worked
tofu output -raw admin_initial_password    # read once, store in password manager
tofu output -raw admin_user_id             # needed for the §5.4 gate
```

### 5.4 Revoke the credential (mandatory — and only once §5.4.0 holds)

#### 5.4.0 Precondition: a verified human IAM_OWNER must already exist

Revoking `iam-admin` removes the **last instance-level credential** unless a
human one already exists. The account tofu just created (`admin.tf`) is an
**`ORG_OWNER`, and `ORG_OWNER` is not enough**: it administers the community
org and gets a flat `403` on `/admin/v1/*`. That is not a corner case you can
postpone — the following all live at instance level and cannot be done by any
`ORG_OWNER`:

- **SMTP / notification providers** (`/admin/v1/smtp/...`) — without them
  ZITADEL sends no mail at all: no invitations, no password resets, no
  verification codes;
- **login and password policies, lockout, MFA enforcement** as instance
  defaults;
- **instance-wide external IdPs** (Google, OIDC) and the login-v2 feature flags
  (`patterns/zitadel-external-idp/`, `patterns/zitadel-login-v2/`);
- **creating further organisations**, instance domains, and
- **appointing further instance administrators** — including the way back out
  of this hole.

Get this wrong and nothing breaks today. It breaks in six weeks, the first time
someone tries to configure SMTP, and by then the credential that could have
fixed it is shredded.

**Recommended: give the account you actually use instance rights**, so the
instance administrator is a person with a name rather than the anonymous
default account from §5.2:

```bash
ADMIN_USER_ID=$(tofu output -raw admin_user_id)     # from §5.3
curl -fsS -X POST "$ZID/admin/v1/members" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d "$(jq -n --arg u "$ADMIN_USER_ID" '{userId:$u,roles:["IAM_OWNER"]}')"
```

**Gate — all three must hold before you continue. Do not revoke otherwise:**

```bash
# (a) read the list and pick the HUMAN instance administrator you will keep.
#     iam-admin and login-client are MACHINES — they do not satisfy this gate.
curl -fsS -X POST "$ZID/admin/v1/members/_search" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"query":{"limit":100}}' \
  | jq -r '.result[] | [.preferredLoginName, (.roles|join(","))] | @tsv'

HUMAN_LOGIN='admin@…'      # ← from that list, an account you can name
KNOWN_PW='…'               # ← its password: §5.2's $NEW_PW, or
                           #    `tofu output -raw admin_initial_password`

# (b) you really hold a working password for it — generated for THIS instance,
#     never a value out of any vendor's documentation
[ "$(probe_password "$HUMAN_LOGIN" "$KNOWN_PW")" = "201" ] \
  && echo "human IAM_OWNER verified" \
  || echo "FAIL: you do not actually hold a working human IAM_OWNER password"

# (c) no vendor default authenticates anywhere (inv. 9)
[ "$(probe_password "zitadel-admin@zitadel.id.{{ domain }}" 'Password1!')" = "201" ] \
  && echo "FAIL: §5.2 did not take — DO NOT REVOKE" \
  || echo "gate clear"
```

`probe_password` is the helper from §5.2. If (a) or (b) fails, fix it **now**,
while `iam-admin` still works — after the revocation there is no route back
except a chart upgrade that re-runs the setup job.

#### 5.4.1 Revoke, prove, then destroy

**Order matters: revoke → prove the revocation while you still hold the key →
destroy the key material last.** Destroying the JWT profile first (the obvious
order) leaves you with two HTTP 200s and no way to test anything: you can no
longer authenticate, so you cannot tell a revoked credential from a working
one. The error direction here is asymmetric — a revocation that silently did
not take leaves a **valid IAM-owner credential** in the instance, and nobody
finds out. Do not continue past a failing verify in this step.

The `zitadel_jwt` / `zitadel_token` helpers are the ones defined in §5.2.

**1. Revoke the key and deactivate the machine user:**

```bash
TOKEN=$(zitadel_token | jq -r .access_token)
USER_ID=$(jq -r .userId "$PROFILE"); KEY_ID=$(jq -r .keyId "$PROFILE")
curl -fsS -X DELETE "$ZID/management/v1/users/$USER_ID/keys/$KEY_ID" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN"
curl -fsS -X POST "$ZID/management/v1/users/$USER_ID/_deactivate" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN"
```

Both answer HTTP 200 with a ZITADEL change sequence. **Two 200s are not
proof** — they say the API accepted the calls, not that the credential is dead.
(Same thing via console: Instance → Users → Service Users → `iam-admin` → Keys
→ delete, then deactivate the user. The negative test below is required either
way.)

**2. Verify — negative test, run it while the JWT profile still exists:**

```bash
zitadel_token                        # read it: must be an error, not a token
zitadel_token | jq -e 'has("access_token")' >/dev/null \
  && echo "FAIL: revoked credential still mints tokens — DO NOT CONTINUE" \
  || echo "revocation verified: the credential can no longer authenticate"
```

Read the first line, do not just run it: an empty response or an HTML error
page also takes the "verified" branch, and that proves nothing — it means the
request never reached ZITADEL. A pass is ZITADEL answering with an OAuth error
(`invalid_grant` / key not found).

If it still mints an `access_token`: **stop**. The revocation did not take —
re-check that you targeted the right `userId`/`keyId`, repeat step 1, and do
**not** destroy the profile in the meantime. It is the only thing that lets you
test again.

**3. Only now destroy the key material:**

```bash
shred -u "$PROFILE" "$PROFILE.pem" 2>/dev/null || rm -f "$PROFILE" "$PROFILE.pem"
kubectl -n zitadel delete secret iam-admin
```

**Verify:**

```bash
test ! -e "$PROFILE" && test ! -e "$PROFILE.pem" && echo "local credential gone"
kubectl -n zitadel get secret iam-admin   # → Error ... "iam-admin" not found
```

A later ZITADEL chart upgrade re-runs the setup job and may recreate
`zitadel/iam-admin` — check for it after every upgrade and delete it again.
That is also the way back to a bootstrap credential if you ever need §5.1
again. If the secret is gone and no setup job will run (rebuild case), mint an
operator service-user PAT in the console instead — `tofu/zitadel/README.md`.

Log in at `https://id.{{ domain }}` as `admin` with that password (forced
change on first login), then do all further identity work via the console /
API — `runbooks/zitadel-identity-via-api.md`.

### 5.5 Hand over an administrator list, not an assumption

The bootstrap is only finished when you can **name every account with
instance-level rights**, with its type and state. Re-run the inventory from
§5.2 — after the revocation the Postgres route is the one that still works —
and put its output in the handover:

```
<id>|admin@<org>.{{ domain }}|human|active|IAM_OWNER
<id>|zitadel-admin@zitadel.id.{{ domain }}|human|inactive|IAM_OWNER
<id>|iam-admin|machine|inactive|IAM_OWNER
<id>|login-client|machine|active|IAM_LOGIN_CLIENT
```

Anything on that list you cannot explain is an incident. Repeat the inventory
after **every ZITADEL chart upgrade**: a re-run setup job recreates
`zitadel/iam-admin`, and a new instance would recreate the default human too
(invariant 9).

## 6. ForwardAuth front door (optional but recommended)

Follow `patterns/app-forwardauth/README.md` — seal the oauth2-proxy
credentials from the tofu outputs, register the pattern, push.

**Verify:**

```bash
curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{redirect_url}\n' \
  "https://auth.{{ domain }}/oauth2/start?rd=https://{{ domain }}/"
# → https://id.{{ domain }}/...
```

## 7. (Optional) CI deploys via GitHub Actions OIDC

With Argo CD, deployments are **pull-based**: production rollouts and PR
previews are reconciled by Argo CD from git (`patterns/app-preview-prs/`), so an
app's CI needs **no** cluster access at all — it only builds and pushes images.
This section is therefore optional, for the rare app that genuinely needs to
`kubectl apply` into its own namespace (it then gets the namespace-scoped
`preview-deployer` role, never cluster-admin).

To let such a CI authenticate without a stored kubeconfig, configure the k3s
API server as an OIDC consumer of GitHub Actions tokens. In
`/etc/rancher/k3s/config.yaml`:

```yaml
kube-apiserver-arg:
  - "oidc-issuer-url=https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com"
  - "oidc-client-id={{ project_name }}"
  - "oidc-username-claim=sub"
  - "oidc-groups-claim=repository"
tls-san:
  - api.{{ domain }}
```

```bash
sudo systemctl restart k3s
```

Then open 6443 — **in the §1.2 nft guard, which is what actually filters on
this node.** `ufw allow 6443/tcp` does nothing here (§1.2): k3s's chains run
before ufw's, so the port was never closed by ufw in the first place and the
rule only creates the impression of a deliberate exception. Edit
`/etc/nftables.d/k3sguard.nft` and accept 6443 *above* the drop rule:

```nft
        tcp dport 6443 accept
        tcp dport { 10248, 10249, 10250, 10256, 10257, 10258, 10259 } drop
```

```bash
sudo nft -f /etc/nftables.conf
```

**Verify from an external host** (see the two traps in §1.2 — a proxied scan
reports everything open):

```bash
nc -vz -w3 {{ server_ipv4 }} 6443    # now open
nc -vz -w3 {{ server_ipv4 }} 10250   # still closed — only 6443 was punched
```

Source `Anywhere` is acceptable for 6443: GitHub's runner IPs rotate and the
API enforces OIDC auth — unauthenticated requests get 401. It is acceptable
for **6443 only**; 10250 and 8472 stay closed, they have no such
authentication in front of them. If the hole is missing, CI deploys fail with
`dial tcp {{ server_ipv4 }}:6443: i/o timeout`.

CI identities authenticate as
`https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com#repo:<owner>/<repo>:<ref>`. Bind
such a subject to the `preview-deployer` ClusterRole with a namespace-scoped
RoleBinding (the `cluster/rbac/` role exists for exactly this). Never bind them
to cluster-admin (invariant) — the policy in `cluster/policy/` denies it anyway.

**Verify:** a `kubectl --token=<github-oidc-token>` from a workflow can
deploy into its app namespace and nothing else.

## 8. Done — handover to normal operation

```bash
kubectl -n argocd get applications    # everything Synced/Healthy
kubectl get pods -A | grep -vE 'Running|Completed'   # empty
```

From here on: changes happen via git (`AGENTS.md`), apps via
`scripts/new-app.sh`, identities via `tofu/zitadel/`. If anything bites you
during bootstrap that this runbook did not predict: fix the runbook, and if
the lesson generalizes, PR it back to the template.
